# Monitor Azure DevOps CI after push

After **`git push origin dev`** or an Azure mirror sync that triggers pipelines for **alert** / **monitoring-app-v2** / **people-analytics-sync**, **do not stop at “pushed.”** Poll builds until **completed + succeeded** (or blocked only by something only the user can fix).

## Pipelines (org `leetclub`, project `Leet Monitor`)

| Product folder | Pipeline | Definition ID |
|----------------|----------|---------------|
| `apps/alert-theleetclub-com/` | `alert-ci` | **3** |
| `monitoring-app-v2/` | `monitoring-app-v2-ci` | **2** |
| `people-analytics-sync/` | `people-analytics-api-ci` | **4** |

Monitor **only** pipelines for products that changed in that push/sync batch.

## How to poll (WSL bash — prefer over PowerShell)

```bash
cd /mnt/c/Users/mahdi/OneDrive/theleetclub/monitoring-app
bash scripts/ado-latest-build-status.sh <definitionId>   # e.g. 3 for alert-ci
```

Auth is via `scripts/ado-curl-auth.sh` (PAT in `azure.txt`). Related helpers: `ado-build-timeline.sh`, `ado-list-build-logs.sh`, `ado-fetch-log.sh`, `ado-build-log-snippet.sh`.

Poll every ~30–60s while `status` is `notStarted` / `inProgress`. Done when `status=completed` and `result=succeeded`.

## On failure

1. Pull timeline/logs for the failed build; identify the root cause.
2. Fix in the monorepo, **commit**, run **`scripts/sync-changed-azure-repos.sh`** (or the matching `sync-to-azure-dev.sh`), then **`git push origin dev`**.
3. Re-poll the affected definition(s) until green.

Do **not** treat push alone as CI success. Prefer WSL bash + jq for ADO curl (avoid PowerShell quoting issues).
